  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-MNG154</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved labour administration, labour inspection, social dialogue, and collective bargaining that enhance  working conditions, including wages, across various forms of employment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The revised Labour Law has come into effect from January 1, 2022, presenting new opportunities and challenges for effective regulation of working conditions. It provides for greater role for the mechanisms and processes of industrial relations and labour administration in achieving decent work outcomes and improved compliance. 

Under the revised Labour Law, tripartite dispute settlement committees have been established at provincial and district level. The ILO has supported the Ministry, workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations to develop procedures for rights and interest dispute settlements. Dispute settlement guidelines were developed, and capacity development trainings were organized for members of the tripartite dispute settlement committees. 

The skills and knowledge of members of labour dispute settlement tripartite committees and conciliators/mediators are developed and enhanced to align with updated labour legislation.
A regular training mechanism is put in place for new and in-service labour inspectors using ILO&#x2019;s labour inspectors&#x2019; training module. The ILO&#x2019;s strategic compliance planning tool is adopted in labour inspection in Mongolia. 

Labour inspection capacity is developed through an institutionalized training mechanism using ILO&#x2019;s labour inspectors&#x2019; training module. Mongolia uses the ILO&#x2019;s strategic compliance planning tool for labour inspection.</narrative>
  </description>
